WEBVTT

00:00.371 --> 00:03.706
You can already write code or
you already live in the data.

00:04.071 --> 00:05.893
You just don't carry the title yet.

00:06.332 --> 00:09.321
I'm not gonna make you sit through
things that you already own.

00:10.128 --> 00:13.256
Bridge is for analysts and
software engineers that are

00:13.256 --> 00:14.589
crossing over into engineering.

00:14.943 --> 00:17.843
It opens with a thirty-minute
placement diagnostic.

00:18.068 --> 00:22.470
Four short tasks, a window function
query with a tie that's planted in

00:22.477 --> 00:25.860
the data so that I could see whether
you know the difference between rank

00:25.860 --> 00:32.472
and row number, a messy CSV reconciled
against a clean API, a wide order file

00:32.504 --> 00:37.988
turned into a star schema with grain
stated in just one sentence, and how

00:37.988 --> 00:40.468
you'd branch, test, and deploy a change.

00:41.043 --> 00:44.792
That routes you to the analyst
track or the SWE track.

00:45.117 --> 00:50.277
Analyst, the gap is usually
engineering rigor: Git, Python, CI/CD.

00:50.783 --> 00:54.665
Engineers, it's usually SQL
depth and data modeling.

00:55.030 --> 00:58.388
You skip what you know, and
you go deep on what you don't.

00:58.884 --> 01:04.007
About three to five months, and faster
if the diagnostic lets you skip more.

01:04.885 --> 01:10.940
Both tracks converge on the same lakehouse
core and the same capstone, a portfolio

01:10.943 --> 01:13.042
project that ends in golden records.

01:13.256 --> 01:16.860
And every week, you're gonna be
defending your decisions on the hot

01:16.860 --> 01:21.648
seat because knowing it and being able
to defend it are two different things.

01:22.107 --> 01:23.321
Take the diagnostic.

01:23.492 --> 01:24.765
Let's find your gaps
